# Normalise line endings in the repository, not on each machine.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on this Windows checkout and there was **no
# `.gitattributes`**, so git converted every committed LF to CRLF on checkout.
# Prettier's default `endOfLine` is `"lf"`, so `prettier --check .` — the *first*
# step of `ci:check` — reported **47 of 47 flagged text files as unformatted** on
# a machine where not one of them was.
#
# The consequence is a repository that cannot pass its own CI on Windows or
# macOS, and that produces a 47-file diff the moment someone formats on the wrong
# platform. Both directions are bad, and the cause is the same: **line endings
# were a property of the checkout rather than of the repository.**
#
# `text=auto` plus `core.eol=lf` below is the standard pairing. The `* text=auto`
# lets git detect text vs binary; the `[eol]` block pins the working-tree form for
# the file types where a CRLF is never wanted.
#
# ## The rule that matters most
#
# `*.bat` and `*.cmd` are pinned to CRLF because **Windows will not run a batch
# file with LF line endings** — it fails with a syntax error on the first line.
# The mirror image is `*.sh`, pinned to LF because a CRLF in a shell script makes
# the shebang line `#!/bin/sh\r`, which the kernel does not recognise, and the
# script is executed as a shell command instead of a script. Both are silent
# failures on the *other* platform, which is precisely why they are written down
# here rather than left to each developer's editor.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch: must be CRLF or Windows cannot execute it at all.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts: must be LF, or the shebang carries a stray CR and the kernel
# refuses to run it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# These are read by tools that are line-ending sensitive and where a CRLF has
# caused a real failure in this repository: a JSON manifest compared byte-wise,
# and the SQL migrations whose `--> statement-breakpoint` markers must match.
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonc text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Binary. Never converted, whatever the platform.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
